{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:outside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:5cc2753cb0a34f0ee71e643fd1ab222cf19cd11f8234ab5262cbde6286499bcd","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.8},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.1},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.4}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Mandated reference class: G7 10y real yield distribution by decade. Approximate GDP-weighted G7 10y real yields: 1970s ~-1.0% to 0% (ex-post negative; the inflation-surprise decade), 1980s ~+4.5%, 1990s ~+4.0%, 2000s ~+2.0%, 2010-2019 ~+0.3% (US ~0.6%, Bunds negative late, JGB real ~0), 2020-2024 ~+0.3% rising to ~+1.1-1.3% by 2024-25. Long-run 1960-2024 mean ~+2.0-2.5%. Starting point, built from the locked prior: US 10y TIPS 2.60% (FRED DFII10, Oct 30 2026) at a ~50% G7 GDP weight, with Japan ~0.6%, Germany ~1.4%, UK ~1.8%, France ~1.8%, Italy ~2.4%, Canada ~1.9% -> GDP-weighted start ~2.0%. Second mandated base rate: does a decade-average real yield stay near its starting level? No -- decade-over-decade changes in the G7 average have been large (-1 -> +4.5 -> +4.0 -> +2.0 -> +0.3 -> +1.1), mean absolute decade change ~1.5-2.0 pp. But at the 5-10 year horizon the level is highly persistent (estimated half-lives of real-rate deviations 5-15 years), so the best single predictor of the 2032-2036 average is the 2026 level, with wide error. Central: anchor at 2.0%, apply a small upward tilt. The tilt is arithmetic, not mechanism: 2A books gross public debt >100% of GDP by 2029 (~142% US, ~127% China by 2031), net interest 2%->3% of GDP, P95 Debt-at-Risk 117% of GDP, eroding sovereign convenience yields, and -- critically -- the IMF's own statement that aging worsens global (r-g) by ~+1.0 pp even where a frictionless r* eases. Supply arithmetic of that size has historically coincided with a higher term premium, not a lower one. P50 = 2.1%. Spread: sigma ~1.0-1.1 pp. P90 = 3.4%: the base rate of a 5-year G7 real-yield average >=3.4% is ~15-20% of post-1970 windows (the 1980s and early-1990s cluster), which from a 2.0% start is an upward move of 1.4 pp -- less than one historical decade-change. I keep it at the 10th-from-top percentile because getting there from 2.0% requires either a Volcker-style disinflation or a genuine term-premium repricing, not both. P10 = 0.8%: requires a return to the 2010s regime -- QE, a demand shortfall, a capex bust pushing r* down. That regime occupied the whole 2010-2021 period, i.e. a large share of recent windows, so 10% is if anything stingy; the reason I do not go higher is that the fiscal arithmetic is a one-way ratchet on supply and the 2010s regime also required a central-bank bid for duration that the locked debt path makes less likely to be redeployed at scale. Final: 0.8 / 2.1 / 3.4.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.097Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD area harmonised unemployment rate (FRED OECDLRHUTTTTSTSAQ / OECD MEI) printing a 12-month average at or below 4.6% with no OECD-area recession dated in the interval -- i.e. the demographic labour-scarcity floor arriving faster than the cyclical reference class allows.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct A moves from 45% toward 65-70%, lifting CQ1 from 16 to roughly 24-26. It also raises CQ2 conjunct A (wage-driven services inflation) by ~5 points and shifts CQ6 mass from D toward B and C by ~4 points combined. It does NOT move CQ3, since tighter labour with a shrinking headcount is the same output.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A full 5-year realised print of PPP-weighted (OECD-38 + China) real GDP growth for 2026-2030 at or above 2.6%/yr on IMF WEO constant-PPP data, with China's own 2028-2030 average at or above 4.0%/yr.\",\"by_when\":\"Apr 30, 2032 (IMF WEO publishing the 2031 actual)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the cleanest falsifier of my central claim, because it would show the China-weight-times-deceleration arithmetic I leaned on by ~110bp is not binding. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.9% to ~2.5% and the whole distribution shifts up ~60bp; CQ6-A moves from 19 to ~34, B stays ~47, C and D fall to ~11 and ~8. My entire departure from the 85-90% historical base rate for band A would be invalidated.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (US DFII10 plus euro-area, UK, Japan and Canada 10y nominal less 10y inflation swap) averaging below 1.0% over any rolling 24 months, with US net interest outlays still at or above 3% of GDP in the same period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my persistence-plus-supply-tilt anchor: it would show the debt arithmetic does not transmit to real yields (convenience yield intact, or a renewed central-bank duration bid). CQ4 P50 moves from 2.1% to ~1.3% and P90 from 3.4% to ~2.6%; CQ5 conjunct A (MOVE >=105) falls from 45% to ~32%, dragging CQ5 from 33 to ~24; CQ6-D rises ~5 points at C's expense. Asset matrix: G7 long nominal bonds in regime C would need marking up and gold marking down.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough decline of 25% or more during which G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields fall by 100bp or more -- i.e. the flight-to-quality bid working cleanly through a large drawdown in a post-2026 high-debt, high-real-rate setting.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Consumes the most likely qualifying episode of the window with a NO. CQ5 conjunct B falls from 48% to roughly 28% (only a later, second drawdown could qualify), taking CQ5 from 33 to ~20. It also raises the equity-bond diversification value underpinning my regime-C/regime-D bond spread, narrowing the C-vs-D gap for G7 long nominals from 10 pp to ~6 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD or Eurostat structural business/earnings statistics showing cumulative real median hourly earnings growth in ISIC F+Q exceeding ISIC J+K+M by 5.0 pp or more over 2025-2031 across a majority of the OECD-38 by GDP weight (i.e. half the required 11-year differential delivered in six years).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032 (on data through 2031)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct B moves from 22% to ~55%, taking CQ1 from 16 to roughly 30-33. It also raises CQ6-B at A's and D's expense (the barbell's defining wage signature) by ~5 points, and raises the locked AI-Q5 marginal's implied value further above 38.2%. Note this signpost's absence by 2032 would NOT confirm my view, since the differential could be back-loaded.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 outside seat, effort 'growth'."}}